status update likely runs every frame comparing RAM to a cached copy. Let me just TEST whether writing updates the HUD. To test, I need a candidate. \n\nLet me generate candidates via narrowing: \n- Snapshot r0 now (0 rupees, on tree screen).\n- Snapshot r1 after varied play (e.g., kill/walk) - rupees still 0.\n- diff unchanged → keeps most (2048 minus ~200). Not enough.\n- Add \"equals 0\"? That's ~1500 bytes. Useless.\n\nNot good.\n\nOK here's the killer method: **trace_writes to find the HUD update routine.** When the HUD needs to redraw (e.g., after taking damage), the game writes to the nametable via $2007. If I trace writes to the PPU data register $2007 (CPU space, address 0x2007), I'll catch the HUD drawing routine's PC. Then disassemble around it to find which RAM address holds the rupee/heart values (it reads them).\n\ntrace_writes address=0x2007, mem_type=cpu, mode=write, frames=... but during normal overworld play without HUD changes, $2007 gets written constantly for room rendering. During gameplay on a static screen, $2007 writes are rare except HUD updates. So: go to a static screen, trace $2007 writes for 60 frames while taking damage or collecting. Hmm.\n\nActually, a cleaner target: trace writes to the PPU address register $2006 or data $2007 while the HUD updates. But there will also be OAM DMA? OAM uses $4014, not $2007.\n\nThis might yield the HUD routine. Then disassemble: it reads a RAM address (e.g., LDA $0679) to get the rupee count. Then patch/test.\n\nThat's a solid plan but requires triggering a HUD update during the trace. Taking damage triggers the heart redraw — easy enough (stand next to an enemy). Collecting a rupee triggers the rupee redraw.\n\nHmm, but honestly this is a lot of work. Let me weigh: maybe just kill Octoroks until a rupee drops — it's the intended path and also naturally gives the HUD redraw.\n\nLet me just fight carefully. I'll use the sword and try to avoid damage.\n\nActually, I just realized something important: *