{"type":"thinking","text":"Interesting: hearts now show 2.5 (one heart damaged: 2 full + partial? Actually \"LIFE\" shows 2 solid + 1 partial/empty). So Link took a hit somewhere. And the Octoroks are all alive (at (95,195),(170,195),(215,195)?). Link at (85,110).\n\nWait, hearts: I see 3 heart shapes but the third looks like outline — so 2.5 hearts. Link got hit.\n\nHmm. Link moved up to y~110. The Octorok that was at (75,105) seems gone — now there are Octoroks at different places. Maybe it moved.\n\nThis is getting messy. Let me use a totally different, more reliable strategy.\n\n**Strategy: use the sword's longer reach and just spam B while walking around.** The concern is dying, but we have the save at slot 1 (tree screen, full hearts). So each attempt can be restarted from slot 1. But the save state slot 1 was taken at the tree screen with full hearts and 0 rupees. \n\nWait — load_state slot 1 restores the tree screen with full hearts AND 0 rupees. Good retry point. But the snapshots r0 (0 rupees) were taken at that same screen. \n\nLet me just keep fighting and check for a rupee drop. Octoroks in Zelda drop rupees frequently (I think ~50%). Let me kill them.\n\nLet me spam B while approaching. Do: [{right+b,8},{right,15}] x many, with occasional up/down.\n\nActually Link is at (85,110), Octoroks nearby. Let me do a loop of sword stabs in all directions: b taps while moving toward nearest.\n\nLet me try: [{down+b,8},{down,20},{down+b,8},{down,20},{right,20},{right+b,8},{right,20},{right+b,8}].\n\nHmm. Actually, the Octoroks at (95,195) and (170,195) are below-right. Let me go down-right.\n\nHonestly, let me just do a broad approach: stab repeatedly while wandering. Let me do 300 frames of alternating directions with b.\n\nActually — WAIT. Better idea entirely!! \n\n**We don't need to collect a rupee to find the rupee address if we can change the DISPLAY by writing.** And to find the address, we can use the DIFFERENTIAL on the *hearts* first, then use the hearts address to find neighbors. But more importantly:\n\n**The Zelda HUD status bar is drawn from a compact block; the counts are consecutive.** Once I find the health byte by differential (which is reliable via damage), I can dump ~32 bytes around it. The rupee/key/bomb counts are almost certainly within that block.\n\nSo focus on hearts differential. I have the tree screen with full hearts saved at slot 1. Let me do controlled damage rounds there:\n\nRound A: load slot 1 (full hearts, tree screen). snapshot h0. Take one hit. snapshot h1.\nRound B: load slot 1 again? No — that resets to full, so the same decrease 6→5 again. That's fine! I can repeat the identical action from the same state: load slot 1, snapshot h0, hit, snapshot h1. Actually to narrow I need TWO independent events with the SAME direction, so:\n- load slot1, snapshot h0, take hit, snapshot h1 → diff h0/h1 decreased → d1.\n- load slot1, snapshot h2, take hit, snapshot h3 → diff h2/h3 decreased from d1 → d2.\n\nSince both rounds start from the same state and do the same thing, the survivors will be addresses that consistently decrease by the damage amount. Zero-page timers may differ between rounds (frame timing), which helps eliminate them.\n\nBut the position of Link/enemies is identical because the state was saved. Actually enemy RNG may differ because the global frame counter differs. Hmm, the frame counter is part of the state. load_state restores it, so it's identical. So a repeat would be nearly deterministic → same survivors. That's fine; the point is the value decreases.\n\nBut the trouble: to \"take a hit\" I need to control it to exactly one hit. Let me define the action: from slot 1 (Link at (12,155)), hold right for a specific number of frames until he collides with something. Earlier, right 130 got him to (85,160) with no hit, then up 55+b got a hit. Hmm, inconsistent.\n\nAlternatively, use the Leevers on the north screen: they home to Link, so standing still guarantees a hit within ~2 seconds. Let me create a save state on the north screen.\n\nActually, simplest controlled damage: stand still on the tree screen and wait — Octoroks wander and shoot rocks. A rock hit = damage. Might take a while.\n\nHmm, let me use the north screen (Leevers). From start, go up. Then stand still. Leevers will hit within a couple seconds. I'll save that state.\n\nPlan:\n1. Load clean file.\n2. Go north (up 100) → Leever screen.\n3. save_state slot 2 (full hearts, leever screen).\n4. snapshot h0.\n5. Wait (empty) ~150 frames → take a hit; screenshot to confirm.\n6. snapshot h1.\n7. diff h0/h1 decreased → d1.\n8. load slot2, snapshot h2, wait same, snapshot h3, diff from d1.\n\nLet's do it. Note earlier the north screen had \"blue/white\" Octoroks — those might be Leevers (they move fast, chase). Actually the blue ones at (90,95),(105,145),(130,140)? They looked like spinning blue things = Leevers.\n\nHmm, Leevers in Zelda 1 are in the desert/sand screens? The screen north of start is a rocky area. The blue spinners... could be \"Zols\"? No. Whatever; they chase.\n\nLet me go.\n\nActually, maybe simpler: on the tree screen at slot 1, the Octoroks are already present, and rocks hit. Let me just wait there. Standing still, an Octorok rock will hit within a few seconds. Let me test: load slot1, snapshot h0, wait 240 frames, screenshot, snapshot h1.\n\nLet me do that; it's less navigation.\n\nBut wait — load_state slot1 then immediately snapshot then play. Fine, sequential calls.\n\nLet me do: load_state slot 1 (call alone), then snapshot h0 and play_input wait.\n\nActually load_state and snapshot can be in the same block? They're independent... but ordering matters (snapshot after load). Risky. Do sequentially.\n\nLet me load slot 1."}
