h = 6 (3 full) the heart tile = full heart tile; when health = 5, third heart = half tile. In OAM, the tile byte of the third heart changes.\n\nFrom d1 (first damage, 6→5), OAM hits included 0x0249 (4→0) and 0x024D (6→2). Hmm, those are OAM bytes at 0x0249 (sprite index (0x249-0x200)/4 = 0x49/4 = 18.25 → not aligned to 4? OAM entries are 4 bytes: 0x0248,0x0249,0x024A,0x024B = sprite 18; 0x024C..0x024F = sprite 19). d1: 0x0248 223→207? no that was e1. d1 had 0x0249 (4→0), 0x024D (6→2).\n\nHmm, but those are probably Link's sprites (position/animation), not hearts.\n\nHmm wait, 0x0249 = OAM sprite 18, byte 1 = tile index. 4→0. 0x024D = sprite 19, byte 1 = tile. 6→2. But those changed with damage AND Link's animation.\n\nLet me directly compare full-health vs damaged OAM. But I have snapshots; I can read OAM now (2.5 hearts) and from a full-health state.\n\nActually, this is getting really deep. Let me step back and consider the practical reality: I need to produce a mapping sheet. Let me allocate effort wisely.\n\n**Pragmatic plan**: Use the beam to kill an Octorok and get a rupee, but do it right. Let me verify the beam works:\n\nLoad slot 2, press B once, screenshot. If a beam appears → good.\n\nLet me also reconsider: maybe I should use the *tree screen* slot 1 where there's an Octorok far away, and use beams.\n\nLet me just test beam firing.\n\nLoad slot 2, then play [{b,4}], screenshot."}
