etter use list_sprites which already decodes. Let me call list_sprites.\n\nLet me think about what the heart sprites look like: 3 hearts at screen x≈165,185,205, y≈30. Their tile indices: full heart tile vs half heart tile vs empty (no sprite). So in OAM, at full health there are 3 heart sprites; at 2.5 hearts maybe the third is a half-heart tile.\n\nIf the change is in the sprite tile byte for the third heart, then the health fully determines the OAM. Fine.\n\nBut how does this give me the health RAM? By tracing writes to that OAM byte's shadow address. The OAM shadow is at 0x0200-0x02FF. If heart sprite #k is at OAM byte 0x02xx, then trace_writes on that shadow byte during a damage event → the PC that writes it. Disassemble → it reads health.\n\nThis is doable. But there's an easier route: the OAM shadow byte for the heart tile. Wait — actually the simplest: trace_writes on the OAM shadow byte for a heart, and the writing routine will be the \"draw hearts\" routine that reads the health variable. Disassemble → find the LDA $xxxx.\n\nOK but let me first find which OAM byte is a heart.\n\nLet me call list_sprites now (2.5 hearts) and look at top-of-screen sprites.\n\nLet me do both: list_sprites and read OAM.\n\nActually — hmm, an even simpler idea just struck me. **The Link's hearts value is displayed; when I take damage the third heart changes from full to half. The tile index (CHR) of that heart changes. That tile index is exactly one of a few constant values. I can search PPU OAM for it.** Not helpful.\n\nLet me just do list_sprites.\n\nActually, wait, I want to reconsider whether the hearts are sprites at all. In the screenshots, the hearts are at y≈30, same row as \"LIFE\". The \"LIFE\" text is background. The hearts look like they could be sprites.\n\nLet me just call list_sprites and see.\n\nI'll also read OAM.\n\nLet me do it."}
